WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.782
Coding Exercise 4 is short, but it tests an important interpretation.

00:00:04.782 --> 00:00:11.052
The wording is “not definitely Paid.” That means a missing status should remain because we cannot say that it

00:00:11.052 --> 00:00:16.362
is Paid. Ordinary not-equal is not enough.

00:00:16.362 --> 00:00:22.572
When NULL is compared with Paid, the result is UNKNOWN and the row disappears from WHERE.

00:00:22.572 --> 00:00:28.622
Use IS DISTINCT FROM so the comparison always produces a definite Boolean result.

00:00:28.622 --> 00:00:33.902
After running the query, you should see the missing-status billing record included along with known non-Paid

00:00:33.902 --> 00:00:41.532
statuses. The main lesson is to connect the operator to the wording of the policy.

00:00:41.532 --> 00:00:46.102
“Known and not Paid” and “not definitely Paid” sound similar, but they are not the same rule.

00:00:46.102 --> 00:00:51.071
Choose the predicate that matches the intended meaning.
